+/* ---- frames in flight (patch 0063, RSX_FRAMES_IN_FLIGHT) --------------------
+ * One command allocator and one set of per-frame rings meant that the walker
+ * could record nothing of frame N+1 until the GPU had finished frame N: on a
+ * GPU slower than the game (integrated graphics at a high Internal
+ * resolution) every frame was game + GPU + the rest of the translation, in
+ * series. With two slots -- an allocator, the upload/vertex/index/constant
+ * rings and the shader-visible descriptor rings each -- frame N+1 is recorded
+ * into the other slot while the GPU still runs frame N, and a slot is only
+ * waited for when it comes round again (frame N+2). Nothing the guest sees
+ * changes: labels, reports, the flip and the user command are written by the
+ * walker as it reaches them, as before, and the game's memory is copied into
+ * the rings at translation time, so the GPU never reads guest memory. What
+ * the GPU of the frame in flight still uses is never touched: textures and
+ * surfaces replaced meanwhile are released only after that slot's fence (the
+ * retired list moves with the slot), every other buffer the CPU writes for
+ * the GPU (present descriptors per backbuffer, the overlay's 8 slots) already
+ * rotates, and a flush that waits for the GPU waits for all of it.
+ * RSX_FRAMES_IN_FLIGHT=2 turns it on; the default stays one slot: on
+ * integrated graphics (one memory for the processor and the GPU) recording
+ * the next frame while the GPU draws made the GPU's passes slower by more
+ * than the overlap saved (4K on the 7950X's Radeon: 23.5 -> 31.8 ms of GPU
+ * per frame, 35-37 -> 31-32 fps; docs/RENDER_PERF.md "4K on integrated
+ * graphics"). A discrete GPU slower than the game is where it can help. */
